WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.455 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.455 --> 00:00:02.425 align:middle line:90%


00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:04.365 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:06.790 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:11.940 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.120 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: All right.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.760 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's continue with our
discussion of linear regression

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.620 align:middle line:90%
modeling.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.760 align:middle line:84%
And as we introduced
last time, there

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.200 align:middle line:84%
are ways one can
formalize fitting

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.020 align:middle line:84%
a model, which is
to propose a model,

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.320 align:middle line:84%
specify criteria for
judging different fits

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.320 align:middle line:84%
or estimators of
model parameters,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.240 align:middle line:84%
then find the best
estimators, and then check

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:46.360 align:middle line:84%
our assumptions underlying the
specification of the criterion.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.680 align:middle line:84%
And possibly, if
necessary, modify the model

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.020 align:middle line:84%
because the assumptions that
we made aren't satisfied.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.120 align:middle line:84%
So we either want to add
additional assumptions

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:06.520 align:middle line:84%
or consider transformations
of the model, perhaps.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:13.800 align:middle line:84%
So with ordinary least
squares regression,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.720 align:middle line:84%
we have this criterion
for specifying

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.120 align:middle line:90%
regression parameters.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.630 align:middle line:84%
And we specify these
in terms of a y vector.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.320 align:middle line:90%


00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:34.200 align:middle line:84%
And this has values of a
dependent variable across cases

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:43.760 align:middle line:84%
in the data, an x matrix, which
will have n rows and p columns.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.120 align:middle line:84%
And we'll have
general elements Xij.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:56.440 align:middle line:84%
And then our model will be that
y is equal to x, the matrix,

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:57.770 align:middle line:90%
times a beta vector.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.600 align:middle line:90%


00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:06.360 align:middle line:84%
So we can have a beta vector
that has p components,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:17.560 align:middle line:84%
and an error vector, which has
n terms corresponding to the n

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.200 align:middle line:90%
cases.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.380 align:middle line:84%
So in specifying the
regression model,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.280 align:middle line:84%
we have that our n
vector y is going

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.960 align:middle line:84%
to be a linear combination
of the columns of x.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.560 align:middle line:84%
And there will be an error
vector characterizing

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.000 align:middle line:90%
the discrepancy from that.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:51.120 align:middle line:84%
So how should we
specify this beta vector

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.666 align:middle line:84%
if we have a least
squares criterion?

00:02:53.666 --> 00:02:57.040 align:middle line:90%


00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:07.680 align:middle line:84%
We can look at sum 1 to n of
yi minus y hat i, where, say, y

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.440 align:middle line:90%
hat is equal to x beta hat.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:22.840 align:middle line:84%
And this criterion is basically
a sum-of-squares criterion.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.320 align:middle line:84%
So it's like a sum
of squared errors.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:33.840 align:middle line:84%
Now minimizing this can be
done just with simple calculus.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.230 align:middle line:90%
We can plug in for--

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.960 align:middle line:90%


00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:44.700 align:middle line:84%
in the formula and have this
sum-of-squares criterion,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:51.640 align:middle line:84%
which is simply y minus x
beta transpose y minus x beta.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:57.090 align:middle line:84%
So we have Q of
beta is basically

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:01.690 align:middle line:90%
y minus x beta squared.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:12.610 align:middle line:84%
So it's the distance of our
n vector from the prediction,

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.010 align:middle line:90%
or fitted value, I guess.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:20.089 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put the beta hat
here to make it explicit.

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:23.930 align:middle line:84%
And we basically want
our fitted values

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:28.850 align:middle line:84%
to be as close as possible
to the actual values.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:34.690 align:middle line:84%
Now because this
criterion is a quadratic--

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.830 align:middle line:84%
it's a quadratic in the
regression coefficients,

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:45.250 align:middle line:84%
we can simply take
derivatives of Q

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.250 align:middle line:84%
and solve for those
derivatives equal to 0.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.850 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
first-order conditions.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:54.230 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:05:00.610 align:middle line:84%
How do we know whether the
solution to this equation

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:07.770 align:middle line:84%
actually minimizes the sum
of squared errors criterion?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:12.490 align:middle line:84%
A first-order condition is
one where the derivative

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:16.050 align:middle line:90%
of the function is equal to 0.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.660 align:middle line:84%
That could be at a minimum, but
it also could be at a maximum.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:25.170 align:middle line:90%


00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:26.350 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You also want to check
the second order derivative

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.733 align:middle line:84%
and make sure that
it's positive.

00:05:31.733 --> 00:05:32.650 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.330 align:middle line:84%
We want to take the
second-order derivative.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And if it's positive,
then we have a--

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:45.450 align:middle line:90%
I guess it's a concave--

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.650 align:middle line:84%
or, I'm sorry, a
convex function.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.530 align:middle line:84%
And so the first
derivative will solve that.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:57.570 align:middle line:84%
Now this is the first
derivative equation.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:07.230 align:middle line:84%
If we were to look at
dQ of beta by d beta j--

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:11.110 align:middle line:90%


00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:11.610 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:12.690 align:middle line:90%
Is that beta j?

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.370 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:26.010 align:middle line:84%
If we were to look at the second
order derivative by d beta d

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:30.370 align:middle line:84%
beta transpose,
then analytically,

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:37.610 align:middle line:84%
we'll basically get
this term times minus x.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:41.250 align:middle line:84%
And so that will end up
being a positive term,

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:46.610 align:middle line:84%
in terms of products of columns
of x and the full x matrix.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:53.530 align:middle line:84%
And so with that, we have
this set of equations.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:07:00.810 align:middle line:84%
Now if you read up on regression
theory, linear model theory,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:05.770 align:middle line:84%
this equation of
the derivative of Q

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:10.690 align:middle line:84%
with respect to beta equaling 0
is equivalent to this equation

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:18.370 align:middle line:84%
here, which is just obtained by
eliminating the minus 2 factor

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.810 align:middle line:90%
and solving for beta.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:25.116 align:middle line:84%
And this is called
the normal equations.

00:07:25.116 --> 00:07:27.770 align:middle line:90%


00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:33.050 align:middle line:84%
What is familiar to some people
is that in statistics theory,

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:40.390 align:middle line:84%
the names of important terms is
often not very sophisticated,

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:44.410 align:middle line:84%
in terms of here we have what
we call the normal equations.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.270 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing really
normal about it,

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.770 align:middle line:84%
but it's a way of specifying
a set of equations

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.050 align:middle line:90%
that we need to solve.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:59.490 align:middle line:84%
And if the X transpose
X matrix is invertible,

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:03.130 align:middle line:84%
then we have a
solution by multiplying

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:06.810 align:middle line:84%
both sides of this
equation by the inverse.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:12.350 align:middle line:84%
And in order for
this process to work,

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:15.060 align:middle line:84%
we need to have that X
must have full column rank.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.770 align:middle line:90%


00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:20.470 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:29.610 align:middle line:84%
So here, if we plug in that
least squares estimate--

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.330 align:middle line:84%
so we have beta hat
equal to x transpose

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.770 align:middle line:90%
x inverse x transpose y.

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:45.630 align:middle line:84%
If we plug that into our
formula for the fitted values,

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:55.770 align:middle line:84%
then this becomes
this formula times y.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.020 align:middle line:84%
And I'll put square
brackets around that.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.970 align:middle line:90%


00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:09.110 align:middle line:84%
And this is called the hat
matrix by some statisticians.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:17.210 align:middle line:84%
Now this matrix, H, is
actually a very special matrix.

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:20.410 align:middle line:90%
It has special properties.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:25.630 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone know what those
special properties might be?

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:30.450 align:middle line:90%


00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.770 align:middle line:90%
Anybody in the back know?

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.450 align:middle line:90%
Kai.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: From memory,
it's a projection

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:40.490 align:middle line:90%
onto the column space of x.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.690 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes, it is.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:43.370 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:52.020 align:middle line:84%
So H is a projection
matrix and it's

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:59.460 align:middle line:84%
onto the column space
of the X matrix.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:06.160 align:middle line:84%
Now what are properties
of a projection matrix?

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:12.100 align:middle line:84%
If we multiply the
projection matrix by itself,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:16.020 align:middle line:84%
we actually get the
projection matrix.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:20.860 align:middle line:84%
And what's relevant
is that if we

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:32.020 align:middle line:84%
were to take Hy hat, which
is then equal to H times Hy,

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:41.020 align:middle line:84%
if this first term is
in the column space of x

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:46.300 align:middle line:84%
as a projection, then projecting
it onto that same column space

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.460 align:middle line:90%
will have no difference.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:54.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is logically
what it should be.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:58.060 align:middle line:84%
And if we multiply out the
projection matrix times itself,

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.630 align:middle line:90%
we get this property.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:04.980 align:middle line:90%


00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:05.540 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:14.280 align:middle line:84%
Well, Importantly in modeling
is to look at residuals,

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:17.020 align:middle line:90%
which are model errors.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:25.640 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have epsilon hat
is equal to y minus y hat,

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.500 align:middle line:90%
this is y minus--

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.340 align:middle line:84%
or y times the identity
matrix minus Hy.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:36.780 align:middle line:90%


00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:45.180 align:middle line:84%
And this matrix
here, multiplying

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:55.900 align:middle line:84%
y to give us our residuals, it
is also a projection matrix.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:59.000 align:middle line:90%
So this is a projection as well.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:02.060 align:middle line:90%


00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:09.020 align:middle line:84%
And if we multiply I minus H
times I minus H and expand it

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:18.580 align:middle line:84%
out, we get I squared
minus 2H plus H squared.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:22.020 align:middle line:84%
And that equals I minus H.
So we can mathematically

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:25.940 align:middle line:90%
verify that it's a projection.

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:33.460 align:middle line:84%
And a very important property
of this residual vector

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:40.960 align:middle line:84%
is that it is orthogonal
to the fitted values.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.620 align:middle line:84%
So in the normal
equations, we have

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:50.300 align:middle line:90%
this equation must equal zero.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.620 align:middle line:84%
This term here is
the residual vector.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:13:00.180 align:middle line:84%
And if we multiply that n
vector by the transpose of x,

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.140 align:middle line:84%
and it's equal to
0, then we must

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:08.410 align:middle line:84%
have that the error vector,
epsilon hat, is orthogonal to x.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.460 align:middle line:90%


00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:16.560 align:middle line:90%
Now let's see.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:20.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure if it's on
the next slide or not.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:23.660 align:middle line:90%


00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:29.660 align:middle line:84%
I think something to keep in
mind that's really quite useful

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:37.180 align:middle line:84%
when we use linear algebra with
least squares is that we have y,

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:43.890 align:middle line:84%
being in n-dimensional
space, the--

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:50.500 align:middle line:84%
we have an X matrix
n by p, which

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:55.620 align:middle line:84%
corresponds to a column
space of x, which

00:13:55.620 --> 00:14:01.140 align:middle line:90%
is a subspace of R to the n.

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:05.180 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:19.300 align:middle line:84%
If we have y hat
and epsilon hat,

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:23.020 align:middle line:84%
these are orthogonal
to each other.

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:30.180 align:middle line:84%
So if we think in n dimensions
of having a y vector

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:46.620 align:middle line:84%
and have a y hat n
vector, then y minus y--

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:47.300 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:49.590 align:middle line:90%
Not y hat, y minus y hat.

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:54.820 align:middle line:90%


00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:59.860 align:middle line:84%
So if we take the vector
from here to here,

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:05.260 align:middle line:90%
that's y minus y hat.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:10.020 align:middle line:84%
OK, this is going to
be orthogonal to y hat.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:15.900 align:middle line:84%
So we actually have right
angles here in between the y hat

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:18.780 align:middle line:90%
vector and the y vector.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:23.860 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, we have-- the y hat
vector and the error vector.

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:38.180 align:middle line:84%
And so from this, we have
that the squared length of y

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:42.500 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the squared length
of y hat plus the squared length

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:44.860 align:middle line:90%
of the residual vector.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:50.500 align:middle line:84%
And then if we expand
this squared length out,

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:53.780 align:middle line:84%
we actually get 0 because
of the orthogonality.

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:57.300 align:middle line:84%
So with least
squares, we basically

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:04.780 align:middle line:84%
have a Pythagorean theorem that
generalizes from two dimensions

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.540 align:middle line:90%
to n dimensions.

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:15.060 align:middle line:84%
And that generalization is
quite convenient and useful when

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:20.260 align:middle line:84%
we think about not the
mathematical exercise of least

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:23.860 align:middle line:84%
squares, but we think about
having probability distributions

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:25.610 align:middle line:90%
on, say, the errors.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:30.180 align:middle line:90%


00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:35.310 align:middle line:84%
So that leads us
to trying to extend

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:41.550 align:middle line:84%
the mathematical regression
model to a probability model.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And so with this, we have
constants assumptions

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.950 align:middle line:84%
for the independent variables
in the x matrix, constants

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.510 align:middle line:90%
for the regression parameters.

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:58.150 align:middle line:84%
But the error
terms, those will be

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:02.630 align:middle line:84%
assumed to follow some
probability model.

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:05.530 align:middle line:84%
What's useful to
think about is, well,

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:08.069 align:middle line:84%
what would be the simplest
possible probability

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:12.030 align:middle line:90%
model to apply to a regression?

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:15.550 align:middle line:84%
Well, we could assume
that the errors

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:22.670 align:middle line:84%
are iid, which means independent
and identically distributed.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:26.390 align:middle line:84%
And we could also,
for convenience,

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:31.390 align:middle line:84%
assume that they are
normally distributed.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:37.670 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a normal
linear regression model.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:44.350 align:middle line:84%
And when we write out
the model equation here,

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:50.370 align:middle line:84%
the model equation basically is
constants plus the error vector.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:54.390 align:middle line:84%
And the error vector,
epsilon 1 to epsilon n

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:58.670 align:middle line:84%
being iid normal, this,
in fact, is consistent,

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:04.430 align:middle line:84%
or is equivalent to a
multivariate normal distribution

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:08.350 align:middle line:84%
of dimension n with a
0 vector for the mean

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:15.990 align:middle line:84%
and a covariance matrix that's
diagonal with constant variance.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:19.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be a
convenient model for us

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:20.310 align:middle line:90%
to work with.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:24.110 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see is
that different inferences

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:28.590 align:middle line:84%
about how important different
independent variables are

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.950 align:middle line:84%
in a regression
model can be judged

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:38.230 align:middle line:84%
using this model as a baseline
model for that evaluation.

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:47.350 align:middle line:84%
So importantly, the
distribution of the residuals

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:57.470 align:middle line:84%
leads us to a distribution of
the dependent variable vector y.

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:04.910 align:middle line:84%
And so in this slide, we use
the notation of a mu vector

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:08.830 align:middle line:84%
for the conditional
expectation of the y

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:12.240 align:middle line:90%
vector given x and beta.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.950 align:middle line:90%


00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:21.090 align:middle line:84%
And we'll define this
just to be a mu vector.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:24.270 align:middle line:90%
That's an n vector.

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:36.240 align:middle line:84%
And we'll define the covariance
matrix of the y vector,

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.830 align:middle line:90%
or the conditional covariance.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:39.330 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:43.710 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be
equal to the product of y

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:55.680 align:middle line:84%
minus its mean times the
transpose of y minus its mean.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:59.790 align:middle line:90%


00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:07.190 align:middle line:84%
And so this is an n
by 1, 1 by n term.

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:11.950 align:middle line:90%
So this is an n by n matrix.

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:22.510 align:middle line:84%
And we'll call this capital
sigma as a covariance matrix.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:27.710 align:middle line:84%
Now for the special
case of iid errors,

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:33.230 align:middle line:84%
this covariance matrix will be
diagonal with constant sigma

00:20:33.230 --> 00:20:34.870 align:middle line:90%
squared.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:42.830 align:middle line:84%
And it will turn out that
this conditional distribution

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:52.030 align:middle line:84%
of y given x and beta will
have a multinomial distribution

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:56.370 align:middle line:84%
with a mean vector mu and
a covariance matrix sigma.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:21:01.430 align:middle line:84%
So an n-dimensional multivariate
normal distribution.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:02.070 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:10.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, what we're going
to do is show results

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:14.870 align:middle line:84%
of what distribution
the y vector is,

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:19.630 align:middle line:84%
as well as show what
the distribution is

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:22.470 align:middle line:84%
of the least squares
estimate is using

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:24.670 align:middle line:90%
moment-generating functions.

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:30.430 align:middle line:84%
So this example
of the application

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:33.630 align:middle line:84%
of moment-generating
functions is really quite neat

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:40.870 align:middle line:84%
because the arguments are
very simple and accessible.

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:52.870 align:middle line:84%
So if we have an invariate
random vector y and a constant

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:57.710 align:middle line:84%
vector t, then the
moment-generating function

00:21:57.710 --> 00:22:04.990 align:middle line:84%
of the Y vector is equal
to the expected value

00:22:04.990 --> 00:22:08.830 align:middle line:84%
of the exponential
of t transpose Y.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:13.470 align:middle line:84%
So this moment-generating
function has--

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.510 align:middle line:90%
is a function of the t vector.

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:22.070 align:middle line:84%
And we just plug
into this formula.

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:27.390 align:middle line:84%
Now because the Y's, in fact,
are independent of each other,

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:33.070 align:middle line:84%
then we have this
expected product of terms

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:35.910 align:middle line:84%
is the product of
the expectations.

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:40.550 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to use
that independence property

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:43.150 align:middle line:84%
where expectations
of products are

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:45.830 align:middle line:90%
products of the expectations.

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:50.270 align:middle line:84%
And then for each
of the Y's, we can

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:56.630 align:middle line:84%
plug in the moment-generating
function for each Yi.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:23:00.710 align:middle line:84%
And that's e to the ti
of mu i plus a half ti

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:02.070 align:middle line:90%
squared sigma squared.

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:05.270 align:middle line:84%
We went through this
moment-generating function

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:09.350 align:middle line:84%
in our section on
probability theory.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:15.390 align:middle line:84%
Now what's important
from this example

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:18.730 align:middle line:84%
is if we take this
product of all

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:20.960 align:middle line:84%
the moment-generating
functions, and then try

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:26.160 align:middle line:84%
to express it in terms
of the t vector, the mu

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:32.080 align:middle line:84%
vector, and the sigma
matrix, we get this formula.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:37.500 align:middle line:84%
And this formula is, in fact,
the moment-generating function

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.480 align:middle line:84%
for a multivariate normal
with a given mean vector, mu,

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:44.800 align:middle line:84%
and a given covariance
matrix, sigma.

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:48.800 align:middle line:90%
So that result is trivial.

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:50.840 align:middle line:90%
Not surprising.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:53.840 align:middle line:84%
What's less
surprising is that we

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:57.000 align:middle line:84%
can use moment-generating
functions

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:02.480 align:middle line:84%
to solve for the distribution
of the least squares estimate.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:09.440 align:middle line:84%
So this first formula here is
the moment-generating function

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:12.240 align:middle line:90%
for beta hat.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:21.880 align:middle line:84%
And in writing that
out, M sum beta hat,

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:26.560 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use a
different argument, tau,

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:28.990 align:middle line:90%
which will be a p vector.

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:32.920 align:middle line:90%


00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:36.960 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be
the expected value of E

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.240 align:middle line:90%
to the tau transpose beta hat.

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:45.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is the definition of
the moment-generating function.

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:56.000 align:middle line:84%
And if we define the
matrix A to be basically

00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:04.960 align:middle line:84%
the premultiple factor of
A that gives us beta hat,

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:09.880 align:middle line:84%
then our moment-generating
function for beta hat

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:14.500 align:middle line:84%
can be written as
this expression,

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:18.840 align:middle line:84%
just plugging in
AY for beta hat.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:27.720 align:middle line:84%
But then we can express
tau transpose A equal to t

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:30.040 align:middle line:90%
transposed.

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:35.440 align:middle line:84%
So this moment-generating
function of the least squares

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:40.680 align:middle line:84%
estimates beta hat
is actually the value

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.640 align:middle line:84%
of the moment-generating
function

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:45.960 align:middle line:84%
of the multinomial
vector evaluated

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:51.600 align:middle line:90%
at t equal to A transpose tau.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:26:02.360 align:middle line:84%
And so we can simply
plug in this value of t

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:09.880 align:middle line:90%
and get our result.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:15.120 align:middle line:84%
And so t transpose mu ends
up being tau transpose beta.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:21.440 align:middle line:84%
And t transpose sigma t
is equal to this product.

00:26:21.440 --> 00:26:24.880 align:middle line:84%
And if we simplify
it, we basically

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:31.320 align:middle line:84%
have a mean vector, beta, for
the least squares estimate,

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:35.360 align:middle line:84%
and a covariance matrix
for the least squares

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:42.440 align:middle line:84%
estimate that's given by sigma
squared x transpose x inverse.

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:47.080 align:middle line:84%
So this is our moment-generating
function of beta hat.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:51.680 align:middle line:84%
And by the uniqueness of
moment-generating functions,

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:55.040 align:middle line:84%
we can recognize
this as a multinomial

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:59.170 align:middle line:84%
with the given mean vector and
the given covariance matrix.

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:01.892 align:middle line:90%


00:27:01.892 --> 00:27:04.440 align:middle line:84%
So we have
multivariate normality

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:06.480 align:middle line:84%
of the least squares
estimates, which

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:11.480 align:middle line:90%
is very convenient and useful.

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:17.920 align:middle line:84%
Now, from this, we end up
getting marginal distributions

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:22.560 align:middle line:84%
that are normal for each of
the least squares estimates.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:28.360 align:middle line:84%
So we're just thinking in this
slide of the j-th component

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:29.760 align:middle line:90%
of beta hat.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:37.160 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's univariate normal
with mean beta J and variance

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:41.920 align:middle line:84%
given by sigma squared times
the j-th diagonal element of x

00:27:41.920 --> 00:27:44.800 align:middle line:90%
transpose x inverse.

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:54.760 align:middle line:84%
And so we get this nice
distribution result.

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:59.620 align:middle line:84%
Now there's more distribution
theory here to go through.

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:02.680 align:middle line:90%
But let's see.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:07.280 align:middle line:84%
Something just to think about
that's, I think, very powerful

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:21.640 align:middle line:84%
is in some examples
where we have this model,

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:26.040 align:middle line:84%
we may have control over what
our independent variables

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:28.080 align:middle line:90%
matrix is.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.680 align:middle line:84%
And in a scientific
experiment, we

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:35.840 align:middle line:84%
might vary different
conditions of the experiment,

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:39.720 align:middle line:84%
yielding y for different
conditions of x.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:46.360 align:middle line:84%
And with these
models, we might be

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:55.480 align:middle line:84%
interested in a particular
column of the X matrix

00:28:55.480 --> 00:29:01.120 align:middle line:84%
and want to estimate the beta
j, the regression parameter

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:03.680 align:middle line:90%
relationship for that.

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:10.450 align:middle line:90%
And so if we had control over.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:18.250 align:middle line:84%
The X matrix, we might try
to make the j-th diagonal

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:24.570 align:middle line:84%
the smallest we can so that the
precision of that regression

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:28.530 align:middle line:84%
parameter estimate is
as small as possible.

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:37.220 align:middle line:84%
And so suppose, I guess, for
simplicity that the X matrix--

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:42.250 align:middle line:90%


00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:46.860 align:middle line:84%
suppose that x transpose
x is a diagonal matrix.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.850 align:middle line:90%


00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:54.450 align:middle line:90%
And here's Cj.

00:29:54.450 --> 00:30:05.410 align:middle line:84%
The diagonal values of x
transpose x inverse will simply

00:30:05.410 --> 00:30:07.290 align:middle line:90%
be--

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:11.290 align:middle line:84%
so if we do a minus 1 there,
we can do a minus 1 here.

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:16.490 align:middle line:84%
Our diagonal entries
will be smallest--

00:30:16.490 --> 00:30:21.970 align:middle line:84%
smaller the more large
the j-th column is.

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:28.890 align:middle line:84%
And so when you think
of regression models

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:34.170 align:middle line:84%
and experiments, we
basically would perhaps

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:38.050 align:middle line:84%
want to have the spread
in an independent variable

00:30:38.050 --> 00:30:43.090 align:middle line:84%
be as big as possible in
order to specify the line.

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:55.450 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of a regression
model of y on X and we have--

00:30:55.450 --> 00:31:06.330 align:middle line:84%
if we have points of x
and y, basically the--

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:14.090 align:middle line:84%
if we look at the sample mean
of the X's and we consider

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:20.050 align:middle line:84%
concentrating our x values
as far away as possible,

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:23.450 align:middle line:84%
that, presumably, will yield
more accurate estimates

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:27.010 align:middle line:84%
of the slope parameter
in the relationship.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:29.730 align:middle line:90%
And so you can think of--

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:37.450 align:middle line:84%
basically, if we observe points
that are far away from the mean,

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:42.930 align:middle line:84%
then those points will pin
down the regression line

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:44.370 align:middle line:90%
the greatest.

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:53.010 align:middle line:84%
And this issue arises
in experimental design.

00:31:53.010 --> 00:31:55.210 align:middle line:84%
And in experimental
design, one can

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:59.090 align:middle line:84%
think of how one can
construct X matrices that

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:01.430 align:middle line:90%
have these properties.

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:06.530 align:middle line:84%
And as you might
expect, the properties

00:32:06.530 --> 00:32:11.690 align:middle line:84%
relate to the eigenvalues
of X transpose X

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:21.170 align:middle line:84%
and looking at methods for
maximizing those eigenvalues.

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:21.790 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:26.870 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can do some
more distribution theory,

00:32:26.870 --> 00:32:33.450 align:middle line:84%
which is to look at what the
distribution is of our error

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.210 align:middle line:90%
vector y vector.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:42.570 align:middle line:84%
But we can also look at any
matrix A times y and look

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:46.290 align:middle line:84%
at the distribution of
that transformation.

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:55.210 align:middle line:84%
It'll be a linear combination of
the y's for each component of z.

00:32:55.210 --> 00:33:00.690 align:middle line:84%
And it will actually have
an m-dimensional multinomial

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:05.210 align:middle line:84%
distribution with mean
value given by A times

00:33:05.210 --> 00:33:09.130 align:middle line:84%
the expected value of
y, and covariance matrix

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:14.530 align:middle line:84%
equal to A times the
covariance of yA transposed.

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:19.010 align:middle line:84%
And so this matrix, A, will
give us the distribution

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.090 align:middle line:90%
of beta hat, as we just did.

00:33:21.090 --> 00:33:27.138 align:middle line:84%
But we can also use different
definitions of and A and z.

00:33:27.138 --> 00:33:32.010 align:middle line:84%
And the normal
distribution theory

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:40.470 align:middle line:84%
leads to these key properties
of normal linear model terms.

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:46.850 align:middle line:84%
We have-- the beta hat least
squares estimate is multinomial.

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:52.450 align:middle line:84%
We have that the error
vector, epsilon hat,

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:54.050 align:middle line:90%
is also multinomial.

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:56.810 align:middle line:90%


00:33:56.810 --> 00:34:06.690 align:middle line:84%
But it's multinomial in n
dimensions with mean vector 0.

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:13.530 align:middle line:84%
So epsilon hat, which
is equal to I minus H y

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:18.810 align:middle line:84%
is distributed as a
multinomial in n dimensions

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:23.530 align:middle line:84%
with a covariance
matrix given by sigma

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:26.650 align:middle line:90%
squared times the projection.

00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:35.370 align:middle line:84%
Now what's significant is that
this covariance matrix here

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:36.659 align:middle line:90%
is not a full rank.

00:34:36.659 --> 00:34:39.810 align:middle line:90%


00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:42.676 align:middle line:90%
So this is nonsingular.

00:34:42.676 --> 00:34:49.590 align:middle line:90%


00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:51.030 align:middle line:84%
Actually, is that
the right word?

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:51.530 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:55.530 align:middle line:90%
It's singular, I guess.

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:56.429 align:middle line:90%
But it's singular.

00:34:56.429 --> 00:34:57.750 align:middle line:90%
It's not invertible.

00:34:57.750 --> 00:35:02.810 align:middle line:90%


00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:06.250 align:middle line:90%
And what does that mean?

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:14.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, that means that there are
linear combinations of the error

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:19.690 align:middle line:90%
vector that have zero variance.

00:35:19.690 --> 00:35:23.650 align:middle line:84%
There's a linear dependence
in the error vectors.

00:35:23.650 --> 00:35:30.770 align:middle line:84%
And so if we consider x
hat and x transpose this,

00:35:30.770 --> 00:35:32.750 align:middle line:84%
and this is equal
to the 0 vector.

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:34.222 align:middle line:90%
This is the normal equations.

00:35:34.222 --> 00:35:37.210 align:middle line:90%


00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:41.250 align:middle line:84%
We basically have that
fixed linear combinations

00:35:41.250 --> 00:35:43.570 align:middle line:90%
of these residuals.

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:46.570 align:middle line:84%
The residual vectors are
identically equal to 0.

00:35:46.570 --> 00:35:53.530 align:middle line:84%
So we don't have n
independent error terms.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:36:02.960 align:middle line:84%
And so because of that, when
we look at, for example,

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:04.580 align:middle line:84%
estimating the
error variance sigma

00:36:04.580 --> 00:36:09.980 align:middle line:84%
squared, well, if we take the
sum of the squared residuals,

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:13.380 align:middle line:84%
that's the trace of
the covariance matrix

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:15.580 align:middle line:90%
of the residual vector.

00:36:15.580 --> 00:36:22.580 align:middle line:84%
And that's equal to the trace
of this projection matrix

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:30.540 align:middle line:84%
I n minus H. And so the trace of
a sum is the sum of the traces.

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:36.220 align:middle line:90%
And the trace of A matrix is--

00:36:36.220 --> 00:36:41.020 align:middle line:84%
product is also the trace of
the product with terms reversed.

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:45.980 align:middle line:84%
So this gives us sigma
squared n minus trace of H.

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:49.420 align:middle line:84%
And we get n minus p sigma
squared for the expected

00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:51.340 align:middle line:90%
value of sigma squared.

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:55.020 align:middle line:84%
And in regression models
with normal linear models,

00:36:55.020 --> 00:37:01.420 align:middle line:84%
we use this relationship
to estimate sigma squared.

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:04.180 align:middle line:84%
Dividing both
sides by n minus p,

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:10.740 align:middle line:84%
we get an unbiased estimate
of the error variance.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:14.140 align:middle line:84%
Now an additional
property that's

00:37:14.140 --> 00:37:20.740 align:middle line:84%
from part C of this theorem is
that the error vector, epsilon

00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:24.980 align:middle line:84%
hat, and the regression
parameter, beta hat, those

00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:30.460 align:middle line:84%
happen to be independent
of each other.

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:35.860 align:middle line:84%
And the independence
follows by looking

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:42.580 align:middle line:84%
at the joint distribution, the
joint moment-generating function

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:51.620 align:middle line:84%
of Ay, and showing that that is
equal to the moment-generating

00:37:51.620 --> 00:37:55.100 align:middle line:84%
function of beta hat times
the moment-generating function

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:57.300 align:middle line:90%
of epsilon hat.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:38:00.460 align:middle line:84%
So the product of
moment-generating functions

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:06.420 align:middle line:84%
allows us to determine
the independence there.

00:38:06.420 --> 00:38:09.020 align:middle line:84%
And when we have
independence of the error

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:12.480 align:middle line:84%
vector and the regression
parameter vector,

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:21.660 align:middle line:84%
then we can compute t-statistics
for regression parameters.

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:25.460 align:middle line:84%
And we end up, as
stated in the notes

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:34.020 align:middle line:84%
there, that tj equal to beta hat
j minus the true beta j divided

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:40.540 align:middle line:90%
by sigma hat times Cjj.

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:46.700 align:middle line:90%


00:38:46.700 --> 00:38:51.100 align:middle line:84%
This distribution will
have a t-distribution.

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:56.420 align:middle line:84%
Now when you studied statistics,
if you took a statistics course,

00:38:56.420 --> 00:39:00.660 align:middle line:84%
you, I'm sure, came
across the t-distribution.

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:09.860 align:middle line:84%
And the t-distribution
is rather remarkable.

00:39:09.860 --> 00:39:14.140 align:middle line:84%
This turns out to be
equal in distribution

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:26.220 align:middle line:84%
to a normal 0 distribution,
mean 0 and some variance divided

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:35.700 align:middle line:84%
by the square root of a chi
squared distribution divided

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:39.700 align:middle line:84%
by its degrees of freedom,
where these two things are

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:42.780 align:middle line:90%
independent of each other.

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:48.460 align:middle line:90%
And so the t-distribution--

00:39:48.460 --> 00:39:53.140 align:middle line:84%
well, who can comment
on what properties

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:55.630 align:middle line:84%
a t-distribution has
compared to a normal?

00:39:55.630 --> 00:40:05.340 align:middle line:90%


00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:09.900 align:middle line:84%
The t-distribution
is symmetrical.

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:11.440 align:middle line:90%
It's somewhat bell-shaped.

00:40:11.440 --> 00:40:21.900 align:middle line:90%


00:40:21.900 --> 00:40:28.460 align:middle line:84%
Well, if this
numerator is a normal,

00:40:28.460 --> 00:40:38.060 align:middle line:84%
and this denominator is
random, it's random near 1,

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:43.160 align:middle line:84%
then we'll have a heavier-tailed
distribution with the t.

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:43.660 align:middle line:90%
And

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:49.860 align:middle line:84%
So we need to be
able to quantify

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:58.660 align:middle line:84%
how significantly different
from 0 the t-statistic is.

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:04.540 align:middle line:84%
So if we have an H0 that
beta J is equal to 0,

00:41:04.540 --> 00:41:08.170 align:middle line:84%
then this distribution
turns out to be--

00:41:08.170 --> 00:41:11.660 align:middle line:90%


00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:14.380 align:middle line:84%
basically have a
t-distribution--

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:21.780 align:middle line:84%
a t-distribution with n
minus p degrees of freedom.

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:29.060 align:middle line:84%
And so we can judge
whether our data

00:41:29.060 --> 00:41:33.460 align:middle line:84%
provides evidence against
this null hypothesis or not.

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:37.740 align:middle line:84%
And we can test other
hypotheses as well, not only

00:41:37.740 --> 00:41:40.220 align:middle line:90%
that the beta j is equal to 0.

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:45.780 align:middle line:84%
We'll be covering this in a
little more detail with one

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:48.740 align:middle line:84%
of the lecture notes that
was passed out for today.

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:53.680 align:middle line:90%
But the-- let's see.

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:56.800 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone know the history
of the t-distribution,

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:58.640 align:middle line:90%
how it was discovered?

00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:02.500 align:middle line:90%


00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:04.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Through Guinness,
the Guinness Factory?

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:05.557 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:42:05.557 --> 00:42:09.460 align:middle line:84%
There was a statistician who was
working at Guinness with quality

00:42:09.460 --> 00:42:10.900 align:middle line:90%
control.

00:42:10.900 --> 00:42:16.060 align:middle line:90%
And he would look at--

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:21.380 align:middle line:84%
he would look at samples, very
small samples of size four,

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:25.500 align:middle line:90%
from a--

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:29.980 align:middle line:84%
so we'll just write
here "Guinness."

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:39.000 align:middle line:84%
He would look at sample, say,
x1, x2, x3, x4 of a measurement.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:42.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure quite what
the quality measure was,

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:55.590 align:middle line:84%
but he would calculate x bar
and the standard deviation of x,

00:42:55.590 --> 00:43:00.350 align:middle line:84%
which is the square
root of the sum of xi

00:43:00.350 --> 00:43:05.970 align:middle line:90%
minus x bar squared over 3.

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:08.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, actually, I think
he did it-- well I'll

00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:10.150 align:middle line:90%
write it in three.

00:43:10.150 --> 00:43:17.150 align:middle line:84%
And what he discovered
was that if we

00:43:17.150 --> 00:43:27.110 align:middle line:84%
look at Z equal
to x bar over sX,

00:43:27.110 --> 00:43:31.730 align:middle line:84%
these outcomes of
the sample means,

00:43:31.730 --> 00:43:35.350 align:middle line:84%
which are suitably
rescaled, should

00:43:35.350 --> 00:43:37.430 align:middle line:84%
follow a normal
distribution because we're

00:43:37.430 --> 00:43:40.090 align:middle line:90%
looking at a z-score.

00:43:40.090 --> 00:43:49.910 align:middle line:84%
This was not distributed
as a normal 0, 1.

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:52.590 align:middle line:84%
There was much
greater variability.

00:43:52.590 --> 00:43:57.770 align:middle line:84%
And so the person
who did this work,

00:43:57.770 --> 00:44:01.530 align:middle line:84%
they wouldn't allow him to
publish under his real name.

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:04.870 align:middle line:84%
And so he published a paper
under the pseudonym "Student."

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:08.670 align:middle line:84%
And so we call this the
Student's t-distribution now.

00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:10.350 align:middle line:84%
But what was really
quite remarkable

00:44:10.350 --> 00:44:13.490 align:middle line:84%
was to see that
in small samples,

00:44:13.490 --> 00:44:21.230 align:middle line:84%
the variability of these kinds
of statistics, in theory,

00:44:21.230 --> 00:44:24.310 align:middle line:84%
should be close to normal,
but in fact are systematically

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:26.710 align:middle line:90%
different.

00:44:26.710 --> 00:44:29.990 align:middle line:90%
And with-- let's see.

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:36.070 align:middle line:84%
With other questions that
come up in regression,

00:44:36.070 --> 00:44:42.430 align:middle line:84%
one can construct an F test
of whether all the regression

00:44:42.430 --> 00:44:48.830 align:middle line:84%
parameters beyond the first
P are equal to 0 or not.

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:53.070 align:middle line:84%
And one can look
at the residual sum

00:44:53.070 --> 00:44:57.270 align:middle line:84%
of squares from
fitting the full model,

00:44:57.270 --> 00:44:59.870 align:middle line:84%
and then the residual sum
of squares from fitting

00:44:59.870 --> 00:45:03.950 align:middle line:84%
a submodel using
just the first k.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:06.310 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, the first k.

00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:08.710 align:middle line:90%
I misspoke.

00:45:08.710 --> 00:45:14.150 align:middle line:84%
And then this F
statistic is a ratio

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:18.830 align:middle line:84%
of normalized sums of squares
and differences thereof.

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:23.150 align:middle line:84%
And this F-test
statistic comes up

00:45:23.150 --> 00:45:29.390 align:middle line:84%
as an analysis of
variance statistic

00:45:29.390 --> 00:45:32.870 align:middle line:84%
where one can
estimate variability

00:45:32.870 --> 00:45:38.190 align:middle line:84%
using the residual sum of
squares from the full model.

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:43.350 align:middle line:84%
And one can then also look at
the residual sum of squares

00:45:43.350 --> 00:45:45.270 align:middle line:90%
from the submodel.

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:53.310 align:middle line:84%
And if both models are true,
meaning beta k plus 1 to p

00:45:53.310 --> 00:45:58.070 align:middle line:84%
are equal to 0, then
these formulas here

00:45:58.070 --> 00:46:01.550 align:middle line:84%
are estimating the same
variance and they're

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:03.670 align:middle line:90%
independent of each other.

00:46:03.670 --> 00:46:10.270 align:middle line:84%
And so we get the F distribution
as the ratio of two chi

00:46:10.270 --> 00:46:13.960 align:middle line:84%
squared distributions that have
different degrees of freedom.

00:46:13.960 --> 00:46:16.750 align:middle line:90%


00:46:16.750 --> 00:46:20.710 align:middle line:84%
Well let's take a look
at some real data.

00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:25.430 align:middle line:84%
The example data set
I want to introduce

00:46:25.430 --> 00:46:28.850 align:middle line:84%
is actually not a
financial data set.

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:31.230 align:middle line:90%
This is a medical study.

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:42.950 align:middle line:84%
But this example
comes up in some work

00:46:42.950 --> 00:46:50.350 align:middle line:84%
by Efron and Hastie, Brad
Efron and Trevor Hastie.

00:46:50.350 --> 00:46:53.990 align:middle line:90%
And they have some books.

00:46:53.990 --> 00:46:58.150 align:middle line:84%
There's The Elements of
Statistical Learning.

00:46:58.150 --> 00:47:01.830 align:middle line:90%
Actually, this is Hastie.

00:47:01.830 --> 00:47:07.870 align:middle line:84%
And then there's-- with Efron,
there's an advanced book

00:47:07.870 --> 00:47:20.470 align:middle line:84%
on computational statistics,
a text that I use in 18655.

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:26.150 align:middle line:84%
And in this data
set, one basically

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:29.610 align:middle line:84%
has a response variable
and independent variables.

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:34.310 align:middle line:84%
And what's really key
to know is experience

00:47:34.310 --> 00:47:36.190 align:middle line:84%
we have with one
regression problem

00:47:36.190 --> 00:47:38.910 align:middle line:84%
extends to other
regression problems.

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:43.230 align:middle line:84%
Just the names of
variables change,

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:45.830 align:middle line:90%
but the same issues arise.

00:47:45.830 --> 00:47:49.910 align:middle line:84%
And so with this data
set, we're trying

00:47:49.910 --> 00:47:57.550 align:middle line:84%
to predict the
lpsa variable as it

00:47:57.550 --> 00:48:04.630 align:middle line:84%
varies with other independent
variables for subjects in a data

00:48:04.630 --> 00:48:05.130 align:middle line:90%
set.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:09.470 align:middle line:84%
This data set happened to
be a data set on patients,

00:48:09.470 --> 00:48:12.630 align:middle line:84%
or subjects with
prostate cancer.

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:20.390 align:middle line:84%
And so with any data set, one
can compute summary statistics,

00:48:20.390 --> 00:48:25.190 align:middle line:84%
which help us detect whether
there are issues with the data

00:48:25.190 --> 00:48:27.070 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:30.750 align:middle line:84%
There's this pairs
function in R,

00:48:30.750 --> 00:48:34.760 align:middle line:84%
which is quite convenient for
just displaying all of the data.

00:48:34.760 --> 00:48:38.540 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
this Pairs Plot,

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:42.040 align:middle line:84%
every pair of variables
in the data set

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:48.400 align:middle line:84%
are plotted in a scatter plot
with the other variables.

00:48:48.400 --> 00:48:51.820 align:middle line:84%
And so along the diagonal, we
have the names of the variables,

00:48:51.820 --> 00:48:56.940 align:middle line:84%
and then we have different
relationships of those.

00:48:56.940 --> 00:49:05.280 align:middle line:84%
Now if lpsa is the dependent
variable of interest,

00:49:05.280 --> 00:49:09.200 align:middle line:84%
if we look of across
this row, we're

00:49:09.200 --> 00:49:12.400 align:middle line:84%
looking at the scatter plot of
different independent variables

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:13.680 align:middle line:90%
with that.

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:17.000 align:middle line:84%
And it looks like the
lca vol is probably

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:19.040 align:middle line:90%
a very strong relationship.

00:49:19.040 --> 00:49:22.400 align:middle line:84%
Some of the variables,
in fact, are discrete,

00:49:22.400 --> 00:49:25.760 align:middle line:84%
and we're able to
see that as well.

00:49:25.760 --> 00:49:29.960 align:middle line:84%
And if we just fit
a simple regression,

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:35.640 align:middle line:84%
we end up getting output
from the regression, which

00:49:35.640 --> 00:49:39.040 align:middle line:90%
consists of an estimate.

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:46.520 align:middle line:84%
We have a coefficient table
with an estimate column,

00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:56.840 align:middle line:84%
a standard error column,
and then a t value,

00:49:56.840 --> 00:50:03.800 align:middle line:84%
and then finally what's
called a p value column.

00:50:03.800 --> 00:50:08.440 align:middle line:84%
This is the probability
that we have

00:50:08.440 --> 00:50:13.000 align:middle line:84%
a larger value of the
t-statistic being observed.

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:18.160 align:middle line:84%
And what's being
tabled here is results

00:50:18.160 --> 00:50:21.800 align:middle line:84%
of hypothesis testing for each
of the regression coefficients

00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:25.440 align:middle line:84%
equaling 0 or not
in the true model.

00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:30.240 align:middle line:84%
So we have estimates of these
different regression parameters.

00:50:30.240 --> 00:50:33.640 align:middle line:84%
We can get standard
errors of those.

00:50:33.640 --> 00:50:39.480 align:middle line:84%
The standard errors
correspond to sigma hat Cjj.

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:45.040 align:middle line:90%


00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:50.580 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I think it's the
square root of sigma squared

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:51.080 align:middle line:90%
Cjj.

00:50:51.080 --> 00:50:53.600 align:middle line:90%


00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:58.960 align:middle line:84%
And then the t-value is
just the signal estimate

00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:08.000 align:middle line:84%
to noise ratio, which
scales in magnitude how

00:51:08.000 --> 00:51:11.600 align:middle line:90%
important those factors are.

00:51:11.600 --> 00:51:17.960 align:middle line:84%
And we can calculate
the p value.

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:18.540 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:51:18.540 --> 00:51:28.160 align:middle line:84%
So if we have, say,
a beta hat j value,

00:51:28.160 --> 00:51:34.360 align:middle line:84%
and it has this t-distribution,
which is centered at the true

00:51:34.360 --> 00:51:46.360 align:middle line:84%
beta j, and the spread is
given by C hat Cj square root,

00:51:46.360 --> 00:51:50.280 align:middle line:90%
we can calculate--

00:51:50.280 --> 00:51:55.360 align:middle line:90%


00:51:55.360 --> 00:52:02.120 align:middle line:84%
we can basically test
whether beta j is equal to 0.

00:52:02.120 --> 00:52:07.760 align:middle line:84%
And so if we set
this equal to 0,

00:52:07.760 --> 00:52:13.440 align:middle line:84%
if we observe a
value of beta hat j,

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:18.240 align:middle line:84%
we can calculate
how likely is it

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:29.560 align:middle line:84%
that we get a beta hat j minus
beta minus 0 over sigma hat Cjj.

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:31.920 align:middle line:90%
This is our t-statistic.

00:52:31.920 --> 00:52:39.400 align:middle line:84%
We can look at calculating
this probability that

00:52:39.400 --> 00:52:41.640 align:middle line:90%
the t-statistic--

00:52:41.640 --> 00:52:45.000 align:middle line:90%
or of getting a greater than--

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:48.880 align:middle line:84%
a larger value of
that t-statistic.

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:55.960 align:middle line:90%
And with this, let's see.

00:52:55.960 --> 00:53:02.200 align:middle line:84%
If we consider
changing the scale here

00:53:02.200 --> 00:53:09.440 align:middle line:84%
to just be beta hat j divided
by sigma hat Cjj square root,

00:53:09.440 --> 00:53:13.960 align:middle line:84%
this is our t-statistic,
then we basically

00:53:13.960 --> 00:53:21.480 align:middle line:84%
have a t-distribution for the
outcome of the least squares

00:53:21.480 --> 00:53:22.480 align:middle line:90%
estimate.

00:53:22.480 --> 00:53:25.080 align:middle line:84%
And we're calculating
how likely is

00:53:25.080 --> 00:53:31.080 align:middle line:84%
it we would get as large
or larger a t-statistic

00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:37.200 align:middle line:84%
if the true regression
parameters were 0 or not.

00:53:37.200 --> 00:53:42.180 align:middle line:84%
Now, in looking at
these estimates,

00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:47.040 align:middle line:84%
one of the challenges in part is
that the scale of the parameter

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:51.280 align:middle line:84%
estimates varies
depending on the units

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:53.480 align:middle line:90%
of the independent variables.

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:56.840 align:middle line:84%
And so in some problems,
we have very diverse

00:53:56.840 --> 00:54:00.360 align:middle line:84%
independent variables
in the data set.

00:54:00.360 --> 00:54:05.540 align:middle line:84%
And the units are a
property of the data set,

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:11.520 align:middle line:84%
but they're not really an
important part of the problem.

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:20.000 align:middle line:84%
And so what we can do is
standardize the covariates

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:28.200 align:middle line:84%
by normalizing them to have
mean 0 and standard deviation 1.

00:54:28.200 --> 00:54:35.140 align:middle line:84%
So if we have our
X matrix equaling--

00:54:35.140 --> 00:54:35.640 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:37.250 align:middle line:90%
How do I want to do this?

00:54:37.250 --> 00:54:45.760 align:middle line:90%


00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:50.800 align:middle line:84%
So if we have P columns
in our X matrix,

00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:56.640 align:middle line:84%
we can basically take
X1 and transform it

00:54:56.640 --> 00:55:06.000 align:middle line:84%
to X1 minus X bar 1
times the vector of ones,

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:10.730 align:middle line:84%
and divide through by the
standard deviation of X1.

00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:13.520 align:middle line:90%


00:55:13.520 --> 00:55:19.280 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll call this
a random vector, Z1,

00:55:19.280 --> 00:55:20.633 align:middle line:90%
of standardized values.

00:55:20.633 --> 00:55:23.730 align:middle line:90%


00:55:23.730 --> 00:55:29.290 align:middle line:84%
And if we do this,
we're basically

00:55:29.290 --> 00:55:35.530 align:middle line:84%
shifting the X's and
rescaling the shifted values,

00:55:35.530 --> 00:55:39.330 align:middle line:84%
it turns out that
our regression model

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:41.690 align:middle line:84%
on the Z-scores or
standardized scores

00:55:41.690 --> 00:55:45.090 align:middle line:84%
are the same as the
regression coefficients

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:49.250 align:middle line:90%
in the original units.

00:55:49.250 --> 00:55:54.830 align:middle line:90%
So it's obvious, I guess.

00:55:54.830 --> 00:56:12.730 align:middle line:84%
But if we have y is equal to
beta 1 X1 plus beta 2 X2 up

00:56:12.730 --> 00:56:18.210 align:middle line:84%
to beta PXP plus
an error vector.

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:28.850 align:middle line:84%
And we consider Zj is just
simply equal to Xj minus X bar

00:56:28.850 --> 00:56:30.700 align:middle line:90%
j times the vector of ones.

00:56:30.700 --> 00:56:33.570 align:middle line:90%


00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:41.210 align:middle line:84%
And 1 over, say, sj, where sj
is equal to the sample variance

00:56:41.210 --> 00:56:42.130 align:middle line:90%
of the Xj's.

00:56:42.130 --> 00:56:52.370 align:middle line:90%


00:56:52.370 --> 00:57:03.810 align:middle line:84%
We basically can substitute in
for each of the X's the formula

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:04.890 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:24.370 align:middle line:84%
So X bar J is equal to sjzj
plus X bar J plus X bar J.

00:57:24.370 --> 00:57:29.530 align:middle line:84%
So this times this is X
bar minus little x bar.

00:57:29.530 --> 00:57:33.130 align:middle line:84%
And this is times
the vector of ones.

00:57:33.130 --> 00:57:40.650 align:middle line:84%
So if we plug-in to
our Xj values here,

00:57:40.650 --> 00:57:46.410 align:middle line:84%
this, then we end up
getting this equation

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:55.510 align:middle line:84%
with the same regression
parameters coming into play.

00:57:55.510 --> 00:57:58.210 align:middle line:90%


00:57:58.210 --> 00:58:06.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, when we fit the scaled
regression independent variables

00:58:06.010 --> 00:58:10.730 align:middle line:84%
model, then notice that
the t-values and the p

00:58:10.730 --> 00:58:15.050 align:middle line:84%
values for these two
regressions are identical.

00:58:15.050 --> 00:58:21.050 align:middle line:84%
So if we pick, say, the
largest t value, svi--

00:58:21.050 --> 00:58:23.730 align:middle line:84%
or that's not quite
the largest lca vol.

00:58:23.730 --> 00:58:27.850 align:middle line:90%
But let's do svi, 2.949.

00:58:27.850 --> 00:58:41.270 align:middle line:84%
If we scroll back to
the original units,

00:58:41.270 --> 00:58:46.950 align:middle line:84%
the t-statistics are identical
across all the variables.

00:58:46.950 --> 00:58:49.130 align:middle line:90%
And so are the p-values.

00:58:49.130 --> 00:58:53.610 align:middle line:84%
So in terms of interpreting how
important different variables

00:58:53.610 --> 00:58:58.210 align:middle line:84%
are, we get the same
judgments of what's

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:02.010 align:middle line:84%
important in terms of
t-values and p-values.

00:59:02.010 --> 00:59:04.670 align:middle line:84%
But what's particularly
useful though,

00:59:04.670 --> 00:59:08.290 align:middle line:84%
when we use the
standardized covariates is

00:59:08.290 --> 00:59:14.690 align:middle line:84%
that the magnitude
of the coefficients,

00:59:14.690 --> 00:59:17.290 align:middle line:84%
when we have the
standardized scale,

00:59:17.290 --> 00:59:23.170 align:middle line:84%
that corresponds to the impact
of one standard deviation move

00:59:23.170 --> 00:59:26.130 align:middle line:90%
of the independent variable.

00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:31.810 align:middle line:84%
So if we have an
age value that's

00:59:31.810 --> 00:59:35.890 align:middle line:84%
one standard deviation
above the mean,

00:59:35.890 --> 00:59:39.010 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't have
much of an impact

00:59:39.010 --> 00:59:41.690 align:middle line:90%
on the dependent variable.

00:59:41.690 --> 00:59:45.850 align:middle line:84%
If we have the svi, a
one standard deviation

00:59:45.850 --> 00:59:53.530 align:middle line:84%
move above a z-score of 1,
then it has a larger impact

00:59:53.530 --> 00:59:55.970 align:middle line:90%
on the dependent variable.

00:59:55.970 --> 01:00:01.770 align:middle line:84%
So standard deviation units it
can be a very convenient way

01:00:01.770 --> 01:00:03.360 align:middle line:90%
to rescale our data.

01:00:03.360 --> 01:00:07.730 align:middle line:90%


01:00:07.730 --> 01:00:09.930 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:00:09.930 --> 01:00:15.570 align:middle line:84%
And judging the quality
of regression models,

01:00:15.570 --> 01:00:20.290 align:middle line:84%
we can calculate
fitted values and pair

01:00:20.290 --> 01:00:22.850 align:middle line:90%
those with the observed values.

01:00:22.850 --> 01:00:27.010 align:middle line:84%
And look at the scatter plot of
observed versus fitted values.

01:00:27.010 --> 01:00:31.810 align:middle line:84%
And this simple
scatter plot will

01:00:31.810 --> 01:00:35.970 align:middle line:90%
have a correlation statistic.

01:00:35.970 --> 01:00:38.290 align:middle line:84%
And the square of that
correlation statistic

01:00:38.290 --> 01:00:42.010 align:middle line:84%
is actually called the multiple
R-squared, or coefficient

01:00:42.010 --> 01:00:44.610 align:middle line:90%
of determination.

01:00:44.610 --> 01:00:48.050 align:middle line:84%
If you're familiar with
simple linear regression where

01:00:48.050 --> 01:00:52.090 align:middle line:84%
you calculate
correlation statistics,

01:00:52.090 --> 01:00:56.410 align:middle line:84%
you can think of the squared
correlation as being useful.

01:00:56.410 --> 01:01:02.490 align:middle line:84%
In multiple regression, we
generalize a single correlation

01:01:02.490 --> 01:01:07.050 align:middle line:84%
statistic with the multiple
R-squared coefficient.

01:01:07.050 --> 01:01:12.210 align:middle line:84%
And so it basically
tells us how predictable

01:01:12.210 --> 01:01:15.910 align:middle line:84%
our dependent variable is,
given the independent variables.

01:01:15.910 --> 01:01:18.650 align:middle line:90%


01:01:18.650 --> 01:01:25.130 align:middle line:84%
Now in terms of evaluating the
assumptions of our regression

01:01:25.130 --> 01:01:29.210 align:middle line:84%
model, there are
regression diagnostics

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:33.930 align:middle line:84%
that we can apply
to fitted models

01:01:33.930 --> 01:01:40.170 align:middle line:84%
and there are a number of
different important measures

01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:44.130 align:middle line:90%
that we can apply.

01:01:44.130 --> 01:01:47.730 align:middle line:84%
And in the stats
package, there's

01:01:47.730 --> 01:01:52.070 align:middle line:84%
this influence measures
general function,

01:01:52.070 --> 01:01:56.370 align:middle line:84%
which gives a table of
different statistics.

01:01:56.370 --> 01:02:04.370 align:middle line:84%
There's this R student for
computing studentized residuals.

01:02:04.370 --> 01:02:08.370 align:middle line:84%
If we have epsilon hat, it is
distributed as multivariate

01:02:08.370 --> 01:02:12.900 align:middle line:84%
normal with mean 0
and covariance matrix

01:02:12.900 --> 01:02:14.990 align:middle line:90%
I minus H times sigma squared.

01:02:14.990 --> 01:02:18.580 align:middle line:90%


01:02:18.580 --> 01:02:24.020 align:middle line:84%
Our residuals may have
very different variances

01:02:24.020 --> 01:02:31.180 align:middle line:84%
because of this I minus H
factor in the covariance.

01:02:31.180 --> 01:02:36.220 align:middle line:84%
And so studentized
residuals basically

01:02:36.220 --> 01:02:43.780 align:middle line:84%
will divide the residuals by
the square root of that variance

01:02:43.780 --> 01:02:45.860 align:middle line:90%
and an estimate of that.

01:02:45.860 --> 01:02:50.140 align:middle line:84%
And that estimate of the
variance that's in the divisor

01:02:50.140 --> 01:02:56.900 align:middle line:84%
leads to non-normal but
t-distributions for the epsilon

01:02:56.900 --> 01:02:57.540 align:middle line:90%
hats.

01:02:57.540 --> 01:03:02.013 align:middle line:84%
So we'll see that
play out in a moment.

01:03:02.013 --> 01:03:03.513 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
other statistics,

01:03:03.513 --> 01:03:08.780 align:middle line:84%
like how much does the
regression parameter change

01:03:08.780 --> 01:03:11.660 align:middle line:84%
if we include or
exclude different data

01:03:11.660 --> 01:03:13.910 align:middle line:84%
points, different
cases in the data.

01:03:13.910 --> 01:03:17.017 align:middle line:90%


01:03:17.017 --> 01:03:18.600 align:middle line:84%
So let's just see
some of the results.

01:03:18.600 --> 01:03:26.060 align:middle line:84%
So here's the student residuals
for this regression model.

01:03:26.060 --> 01:03:33.380 align:middle line:84%
And we have a histogram, which
is unimodal, symmetrical.

01:03:33.380 --> 01:03:44.060 align:middle line:84%
Here is what's called a quantile
plot of the residuals that--

01:03:44.060 --> 01:03:48.560 align:middle line:84%
where the quantile plot
will follow a straight line

01:03:48.560 --> 01:03:52.700 align:middle line:84%
if the data are consistent
with the normal distribution

01:03:52.700 --> 01:04:00.380 align:middle line:84%
model and the t-distribution
for normalizing the residuals

01:04:00.380 --> 01:04:03.380 align:middle line:90%
by estimates of the variance.

01:04:03.380 --> 01:04:09.060 align:middle line:84%
What's rather
useful when you use

01:04:09.060 --> 01:04:12.140 align:middle line:84%
these methods for different
regression problems

01:04:12.140 --> 01:04:19.780 align:middle line:84%
is that you'll see that there's
sampling variability due to just

01:04:19.780 --> 01:04:21.880 align:middle line:90%
the data set we're working with.

01:04:21.880 --> 01:04:23.860 align:middle line:84%
If you were to
collect a new data set

01:04:23.860 --> 01:04:28.500 align:middle line:84%
under identical conditions, you
would get different results.

01:04:28.500 --> 01:04:32.660 align:middle line:84%
These red bands
correspond to bands

01:04:32.660 --> 01:04:34.980 align:middle line:84%
you might expect to
see with variation

01:04:34.980 --> 01:04:37.900 align:middle line:90%
from one example to another.

01:04:37.900 --> 01:04:44.220 align:middle line:84%
Now this here is a plot called
the influencePlot in the car

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:53.860 align:middle line:84%
package, and it plots hat values
versus studentized residuals.

01:04:53.860 --> 01:05:02.610 align:middle line:84%
The hat values are the
diagonals of the H matrix.

01:05:02.610 --> 01:05:07.900 align:middle line:90%


01:05:07.900 --> 01:05:14.290 align:middle line:84%
And what's important about
the hat values is that--

01:05:14.290 --> 01:05:17.160 align:middle line:90%


01:05:17.160 --> 01:05:17.660 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:21.860 align:middle line:84%
If the hat values
were equal to 1,

01:05:21.860 --> 01:05:34.540 align:middle line:84%
so if, say, H sub ii equals
1, then we would have y hat i

01:05:34.540 --> 01:05:38.300 align:middle line:90%
is actually equal to yi.

01:05:38.300 --> 01:05:44.580 align:middle line:84%
And so the i-th case would be
the only case in the data set

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:50.200 align:middle line:84%
that allows you to
estimate that value.

01:05:50.200 --> 01:05:54.620 align:middle line:84%
So that case would have
very high influence.

01:05:54.620 --> 01:05:59.820 align:middle line:90%
So hat values generally are--

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:03.260 align:middle line:90%
well, are close to p over n.

01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:10.420 align:middle line:84%
And lower values correspond
to lower influence.

01:06:10.420 --> 01:06:19.380 align:middle line:84%
And studentized residuals are
given by these magnitudes.

01:06:19.380 --> 01:06:25.580 align:middle line:84%
And I believe what is
drawn in this slide is--

01:06:25.580 --> 01:06:29.980 align:middle line:84%
yeah, it is the circle size is
proportional to Cook's distance.

01:06:29.980 --> 01:06:32.580 align:middle line:90%
And so let's see.

01:06:32.580 --> 01:06:44.340 align:middle line:84%
With Cook's distance, if we have
beta hat equal to x transpose

01:06:44.340 --> 01:06:53.500 align:middle line:84%
x inverse x transpose y, we can
think of beta hat sub minus i,

01:06:53.500 --> 01:06:56.780 align:middle line:84%
which is the least
squares estimate if we

01:06:56.780 --> 01:07:01.500 align:middle line:90%
were to exclude the i-th case.

01:07:01.500 --> 01:07:05.590 align:middle line:84%
So this corresponds to
excluding the i-th case.

01:07:05.590 --> 01:07:08.540 align:middle line:90%


01:07:08.540 --> 01:07:15.420 align:middle line:84%
Then what we have that this beta
hat is distributed multinomial

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:21.740 align:middle line:84%
with a mean vector, The True
beta and covariance matrix given

01:07:21.740 --> 01:07:22.370 align:middle line:90%
by this.

01:07:22.370 --> 01:07:25.740 align:middle line:90%


01:07:25.740 --> 01:07:30.580 align:middle line:84%
We can actually look
at beta hat minus--

01:07:30.580 --> 01:07:33.910 align:middle line:90%
or beta hat i minus beta hat.

01:07:33.910 --> 01:07:37.380 align:middle line:90%


01:07:37.380 --> 01:07:46.460 align:middle line:84%
And then consider
the distance of this

01:07:46.460 --> 01:07:57.260 align:middle line:84%
or the magnitude of
the change in the beta.

01:07:57.260 --> 01:08:04.150 align:middle line:84%
And Cook's distance will
actually correspond closely

01:08:04.150 --> 01:08:12.390 align:middle line:84%
to a Chi squared distribution
because of the relationship.

01:08:12.390 --> 01:08:15.670 align:middle line:84%
So we're basically looking
at the distance of beta hat i

01:08:15.670 --> 01:08:20.189 align:middle line:84%
from the true beta
and normalizing that

01:08:20.189 --> 01:08:24.229 align:middle line:90%
by the covariance matrix.

01:08:24.229 --> 01:08:27.029 align:middle line:90%
Now let's see.

01:08:27.029 --> 01:08:30.710 align:middle line:84%
There's basically a whole
slew of different diagnostics

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:33.270 align:middle line:90%
that we can graph.

01:08:33.270 --> 01:08:35.029 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's
very useful to have

01:08:35.029 --> 01:08:40.870 align:middle line:84%
graphical methods to highlight
what's perhaps important.

01:08:40.870 --> 01:08:51.270 align:middle line:84%
In looking at this plotting from
the car package, or plot.lm,

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:56.470 align:middle line:84%
we can see how the residuals
compare with the fitted values.

01:08:56.470 --> 01:09:02.510 align:middle line:84%
And we don't like to see any
systematic pattern in that plot.

01:09:02.510 --> 01:09:04.870 align:middle line:90%
We'd like that just to be flat.

01:09:04.870 --> 01:09:06.710 align:middle line:84%
How that will
change sometimes is

01:09:06.710 --> 01:09:09.270 align:middle line:84%
that as the fitted
values increase,

01:09:09.270 --> 01:09:13.149 align:middle line:84%
the residuals might increase
in magnitude as well.

01:09:13.149 --> 01:09:17.270 align:middle line:84%
So that would suggest
that the residuals

01:09:17.270 --> 01:09:21.149 align:middle line:84%
are dependent on the magnitude
of the fitted values.

01:09:21.149 --> 01:09:24.550 align:middle line:84%
We can also look at
the normal Q-Q plot

01:09:24.550 --> 01:09:28.670 align:middle line:84%
to see whether the data are
consistent with normal or not.

01:09:28.670 --> 01:09:37.797 align:middle line:84%
And then one can look
at the scale of the--

01:09:37.797 --> 01:09:42.370 align:middle line:84%
the scale of-- measures of
the scale of the residuals.

01:09:42.370 --> 01:09:46.950 align:middle line:84%
So we can take the square root
of the standardized residuals,

01:09:46.950 --> 01:09:53.590 align:middle line:84%
or the magnitude, and see
whether those have variation,

01:09:53.590 --> 01:09:55.790 align:middle line:84%
depending upon whether
the fitted values are

01:09:55.790 --> 01:09:57.550 align:middle line:90%
small or large.

01:09:57.550 --> 01:10:01.150 align:middle line:84%
Here it looks like there may be
a nonlinear relationship there.

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:05.110 align:middle line:84%
When we discover
relationships, then that

01:10:05.110 --> 01:10:12.150 align:middle line:84%
leads us to refine
our model assumptions.

01:10:12.150 --> 01:10:12.730 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:10:12.730 --> 01:10:19.670 align:middle line:84%
This residual plot's
function is actually

01:10:19.670 --> 01:10:23.030 align:middle line:84%
a function that looks
at the sensitivity

01:10:23.030 --> 01:10:28.150 align:middle line:84%
of our linear regression model
to potential nonlinearity

01:10:28.150 --> 01:10:33.390 align:middle line:84%
in the model dependence
on independent factors.

01:10:33.390 --> 01:10:40.030 align:middle line:84%
And so it actually tries
to fit curvature terms

01:10:40.030 --> 01:10:44.030 align:middle line:90%
into the residuals in the model.

01:10:44.030 --> 01:10:45.830 align:middle line:90%
And if there's residual--

01:10:45.830 --> 01:10:49.550 align:middle line:84%
if there's curvature
present in the residuals,

01:10:49.550 --> 01:10:52.750 align:middle line:84%
then we might have
a nonlinear model

01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:56.520 align:middle line:84%
and need to expand with
the nonlinear terms.

01:10:56.520 --> 01:11:00.710 align:middle line:90%


01:11:00.710 --> 01:11:01.290 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:08.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, with this
normal linear model,

01:11:08.430 --> 01:11:15.950 align:middle line:84%
we can consider the assumptions
underlying that minus normality.

01:11:15.950 --> 01:11:19.430 align:middle line:84%
And those are called the
Gauss-Markov assumptions.

01:11:19.430 --> 01:11:25.056 align:middle line:84%
So if we have our
regression model

01:11:25.056 --> 01:11:29.990 align:middle line:84%
for our y vector
and X matrix, we

01:11:29.990 --> 01:11:35.870 align:middle line:84%
set that up as the conditional
expectation is X beta

01:11:35.870 --> 01:11:39.470 align:middle line:84%
and the covariance
matrix is equal to sigma

01:11:39.470 --> 01:11:42.510 align:middle line:90%
squared times the identity.

01:11:42.510 --> 01:11:47.510 align:middle line:84%
And with these
Gauss-Markov assumptions,

01:11:47.510 --> 01:11:50.190 align:middle line:84%
there's a really
important theorem

01:11:50.190 --> 01:11:57.950 align:middle line:84%
in linear models, which is that
the least squares estimates

01:11:57.950 --> 01:12:01.750 align:middle line:84%
of the regression
parameters give us

01:12:01.750 --> 01:12:05.510 align:middle line:84%
the best estimates of
any linear combination

01:12:05.510 --> 01:12:08.230 align:middle line:84%
of the true
regression parameters.

01:12:08.230 --> 01:12:12.710 align:middle line:84%
So if we want to estimate
a parameter theta, which

01:12:12.710 --> 01:12:16.190 align:middle line:84%
is a linear combination of
the regression parameters,

01:12:16.190 --> 01:12:20.910 align:middle line:84%
then if the Gauss-Markov
assumptions are satisfied,

01:12:20.910 --> 01:12:22.830 align:middle line:84%
then plugging in
the least squares

01:12:22.830 --> 01:12:28.230 align:middle line:84%
estimates provides
an unbiased estimator

01:12:28.230 --> 01:12:34.110 align:middle line:84%
that is also the unbiased
estimator with the smallest

01:12:34.110 --> 01:12:36.030 align:middle line:90%
variability.

01:12:36.030 --> 01:12:42.310 align:middle line:84%
And these are called best
linear unbiased estimates.

01:12:42.310 --> 01:12:46.350 align:middle line:84%
And what's important-- well,
I mean as it's framed here,

01:12:46.350 --> 01:12:47.870 align:middle line:90%
it's quite general.

01:12:47.870 --> 01:12:49.250 align:middle line:90%
But think about this.

01:12:49.250 --> 01:12:53.270 align:middle line:84%
If we have the
constants c1 through cp

01:12:53.270 --> 01:13:00.593 align:middle line:84%
correspond to specific values of
the explanatory three variables

01:13:00.593 --> 01:13:02.010 align:middle line:84%
corresponding to
a different case,

01:13:02.010 --> 01:13:07.630 align:middle line:84%
then we can be estimating the
true mean value for a given

01:13:07.630 --> 01:13:11.310 align:middle line:90%
case of the x variables.

01:13:11.310 --> 01:13:18.050 align:middle line:84%
If we have two x values, x
vectors for different cases,

01:13:18.050 --> 01:13:24.830 align:middle line:84%
and we're interested in what's
the difference in mean values

01:13:24.830 --> 01:13:29.710 align:middle line:84%
for those two cases, then we
can have these c's represent

01:13:29.710 --> 01:13:31.410 align:middle line:90%
the difference in the x's.

01:13:31.410 --> 01:13:33.550 align:middle line:84%
And we're estimating
the difference

01:13:33.550 --> 01:13:37.950 align:middle line:84%
in y values, the difference in
mean values for different cases.

01:13:37.950 --> 01:13:42.510 align:middle line:84%
So it really is a very,
very general theorem.

01:13:42.510 --> 01:13:47.110 align:middle line:84%
And while it's a very
wonderful theorem,

01:13:47.110 --> 01:13:50.870 align:middle line:84%
it depends on the
Gauss-Markov assumptions.

01:13:50.870 --> 01:13:58.030 align:middle line:84%
So what we can do is consider
generalizing the Gauss-Markov

01:13:58.030 --> 01:14:05.310 align:middle line:84%
assumptions to, again,
have 0 mean errors,

01:14:05.310 --> 01:14:08.910 align:middle line:84%
but have the covariance
matrix of the errors

01:14:08.910 --> 01:14:15.470 align:middle line:84%
be given by sigma squared
times a capital sigma matrix.

01:14:15.470 --> 01:14:24.750 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of covariance
structure in error terms

01:14:24.750 --> 01:14:29.190 align:middle line:84%
actually arises frequently
in time series modeling,

01:14:29.190 --> 01:14:34.230 align:middle line:84%
where we're ordering
our data by time

01:14:34.230 --> 01:14:39.190 align:middle line:84%
and maybe near values
of the error that

01:14:39.190 --> 01:14:44.350 align:middle line:84%
are observed at nearby times
are more correlated than those

01:14:44.350 --> 01:14:45.850 align:middle line:90%
at faraway times.

01:14:45.850 --> 01:14:48.920 align:middle line:84%
And the covariant
structure of those errors

01:14:48.920 --> 01:14:52.880 align:middle line:84%
might be systematically
represented

01:14:52.880 --> 01:14:58.200 align:middle line:84%
by a multiple of a
known sigma matrix.

01:14:58.200 --> 01:15:04.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, if this is the case, we
can transform our original data

01:15:04.960 --> 01:15:14.360 align:middle line:84%
by premultiplying by the
inverse square root of sigma

01:15:14.360 --> 01:15:18.360 align:middle line:84%
and do the same
for our x matrix.

01:15:18.360 --> 01:15:23.840 align:middle line:84%
And if we do that, we're
basically transforming our model

01:15:23.840 --> 01:15:30.440 align:middle line:84%
to the starred case
where the Y star and X

01:15:30.440 --> 01:15:36.920 align:middle line:84%
star, as we've represented
here, the epsilon star will have

01:15:36.920 --> 01:15:43.280 align:middle line:84%
mean 0, and the covariance
matrix of the epsilon stars

01:15:43.280 --> 01:15:49.120 align:middle line:84%
will actually be diagonal
with constant variance.

01:15:49.120 --> 01:15:53.960 align:middle line:84%
So in this transformed case,
we have the same regression

01:15:53.960 --> 01:15:55.440 align:middle line:90%
parameters.

01:15:55.440 --> 01:16:03.440 align:middle line:84%
We have the epsilon stars with
the Gauss-Markov assumptions

01:16:03.440 --> 01:16:05.000 align:middle line:90%
being satisfied.

01:16:05.000 --> 01:16:08.560 align:middle line:84%
So by the Gauss-Markov
theorem, we just

01:16:08.560 --> 01:16:11.960 align:middle line:84%
can write directly our
least squares estimate

01:16:11.960 --> 01:16:15.400 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the X
stars and Y stars.

01:16:15.400 --> 01:16:18.640 align:middle line:84%
And when we write
that out, it ends up

01:16:18.640 --> 01:16:24.680 align:middle line:84%
giving us this formula for
the generalized least squares

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:26.120 align:middle line:90%
estimate.

01:16:26.120 --> 01:16:31.160 align:middle line:84%
And so if we have
variable errors where

01:16:31.160 --> 01:16:34.400 align:middle line:84%
we know the relative
variation of those errors,

01:16:34.400 --> 01:16:38.240 align:middle line:84%
we can accommodate that
and get best estimates

01:16:38.240 --> 01:16:42.760 align:middle line:84%
with this generalized
least squares formula.

01:16:42.760 --> 01:16:50.160 align:middle line:84%
And importantly, the generalized
least squares estimate

01:16:50.160 --> 01:16:54.040 align:middle line:84%
basically does a
weighted regression

01:16:54.040 --> 01:17:00.160 align:middle line:84%
where we weight cases
proportional to the inverse

01:17:00.160 --> 01:17:04.700 align:middle line:84%
of the variance of
the random variable.

01:17:04.700 --> 01:17:08.440 align:middle line:84%
So if this sigma
matrix, in fact,

01:17:08.440 --> 01:17:11.600 align:middle line:84%
were diagonal with
different variances,

01:17:11.600 --> 01:17:19.600 align:middle line:84%
we would be downweighting
those cases with high variance

01:17:19.600 --> 01:17:26.280 align:middle line:84%
and performing the least squares
computations in a weighted way.

01:17:26.280 --> 01:17:29.420 align:middle line:84%
So that's the generalized
least squares estimate.

01:17:29.420 --> 01:17:29.920 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:17:29.920 --> 01:17:33.500 align:middle line:84%
Well, we'll finish
there for today

01:17:33.500 --> 01:17:39.110 align:middle line:84%
and cover more
regression next time.

01:17:39.110 --> 01:17:45.000 align:middle line:90%